---
import CaseLayout from '../../layouts/CaseLayout.astro';

const devices = [
  ['M1, M2', '120 µm / 20 µm', 'Input differential pair'],
  ['M3, M4', '300 nm / 300 nm', 'Active load'],
  ['M5', '6.5 µm / 1 µm', 'Tail current source'],
  ['M6', '43.25 µm / 1 µm', 'Second-stage driver'],
  ['M7', '93 µm / 2 µm', 'Second-stage load'],
];

const specs = [
  ['Open-loop gain', '≥ 75 dB', '82.55 dB', true],
  ['Phase margin', '≥ 60°', '60°', true],
  ['Unity-gain frequency', '≥ 25 MHz', '25 MHz', true],
  ['ICMR', '≥ 2 V', '2.1 V', true],
  ['Bandgap output', '1.00 V', '1.00 V', true],
  ['Temperature coefficient', '< 100 ppm/°C', '80 ppm/°C', true],
  ['Power', '< 1.5 mW', 'within budget', true],
  ['Supply range', '3.0 – 3.6 V', 'verified', true],
];
---

<CaseLayout slug="miller-ota">
  <section>
    <h2>The build</h2>
    <p class="muted">
      A textbook two-stage OTA is easy to draw and unforgiving to close. The
      first stage is a differential pair with an active load; the second is a
      common-source driver. Cascading them buys gain and costs you a second pole,
      which is where the compensation network earns its keep.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      Miller compensation splits the poles by wrapping <code>C<sub>c</sub></code>
      around the second stage — but the same capacitor creates a right-half-plane
      zero that drags phase down exactly where you need margin. A zero-nulling
      resistor in series with <code>C<sub>c</sub></code> pushes that zero out of
      the way. That pairing is what takes the amplifier from unstable to 60° of
      phase margin without giving back bandwidth.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Device sizing</h2>
    <div class="tbl">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>W / L</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {devices.map(([d, wl, role]) => (
            <tr><td>{d}</td><td>{wl}</td><td>{role}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="muted small">
      C<sub>c</sub> = 1 fF · C<sub>L</sub> = 10 pF · V<sub>bias</sub> = 947.049 mV
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Target vs. measured</h2>
    <div class="tbl">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Target</th><th>Achieved</th><th></th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {specs.map(([p, t, a, ok]) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{p}</td>
              <td class="dim">{t}</td>
              <td>{a}</td>
              <td class="tick">{ok ? '✓' : '—'}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="muted">
      Verified at 0 °C, 25 °C and 70 °C across the supply range. The bandgap
      includes a startup circuit — without one the reference has a perfectly
      stable second operating point at zero, and it will happily sit there
      forever.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Layout and physical verification</h2>
    <p class="muted">
      This is the project where the design stopped being a schematic. The full
      flow in Calibre — DRC for the foundry rules, LVS to prove the layout is the
      circuit I drew, PEX to pull out the parasitics — then back into ADE&nbsp;L to
      re-simulate against the extracted netlist.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      Post-layout is where optimistic schematics go to die: routing capacitance
      loads the compensation node and eats phase margin. Getting the amplifier to
      hold spec with the parasitics in was the actual work, and it is why I now
      draw the compensation network before I draw anything else.
    </p>
  </section>
</CaseLayout>

<style>
  h2 { font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 2.6vw, 1.9rem); margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  code { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.9em; color: var(--accent); }

  .tbl { overflow-x: auto; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 4px; }
  table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; min-width: 30rem; font-size: 0.9rem; }
  th, td { text-align: left; padding: 0.7rem 1rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
  thead th {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.64rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--fg-3);
    background: var(--bg-2);
  }
  tbody tr:last-child td { border-bottom: 0; }
  td:nth-child(2) { font-family: var(--mono); }
  .dim { color: var(--fg-3); }
  .tick { color: var(--accent); width: 2rem; text-align: center; }
  table:has(th:nth-child(4)) td:nth-child(3) { font-family: var(--mono); color: var(--accent); }

  .small { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.78rem; margin-top: 0.9rem; }
</style>
